#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083244 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083056 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/id #uuid "879e41c3-7d67-5821-81dc-f6d3fc664ffd" :question/math-academy-id "e-597" :question/problem "Suppose that we have a curve defined by the parametric equations \n\n$$\nx=2t+3, \\quad y=t^3-4t, \\qquad -\\infty < t <\\infty.\n$$\n\nWhat is the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point where $t=-1?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(-1)+3=1, \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}-4(-1)=-1+4=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(1,3).$ The plot below shows the curve and the tangent line at this point.\n\n![](images/0f/0fa3fc12e83e2a581499680145df9229917c7426e6a3d12c51d88e115a42033e.png)\n\nTo find the slope of the tangent line, we need to find $\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\nx'(t) = 2, \\qquad y'(t) = 3t^2-4 .\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = \\dfrac{y'(t)}{x'(t)} = \\dfrac{3t^2-4}{2}.\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}-4}{2}=-\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $(x_1,y_1) = (1,3)$ and the slope $m=-\\dfrac{1}{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\ny - 3 = -\\frac{1}{2}\\left(x-1\\right).\n$$"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050531} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050644}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083912 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2f6a1fb9-a35a-5b64-913e-c9df98bccbff" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y=\\frac{24}{11}\\,x+\\frac{2}{11}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333430} {:answer/id #uuid "53eed6ee-9b5a-505e-be78-f6bb4c4f4050" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186333753} {:answer/id #uuid "7ece2cb0-9b4f-5f1c-8dff-02cde62728ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} {:answer/id #uuid "95bef049-706d-5788-8e10-cb68b22507f6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334321} {:answer/id #uuid "a6894270-0960-537c-a6f0-979b8b3809d0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7ece2cb0-9b4f-5f1c-8dff-02cde62728ba" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=2(x+1)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186334120} :answer-field/id #uuid "6ac34767-b922-5f65-a668-1c1e1e6c8f45" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186335447}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b94e72be-f23a-535e-b719-cf664553611b" :question/math-academy-id "q-6857" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{3}-3t,\\,y=3{t}^{2}-5,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{3}-3(1)=2-3=-1, \\\\ y & =3(1)^{2}-5=3-5=-2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-1,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=6{t}^{2}-3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=6t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{6t}{6{t}^{2}-3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{6\\cdot 1}{6\\cdot (1)^{2}-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{6}{6-3} \\\\  & =2\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-1,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y+2 & =2(x+1).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186083913 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f56a41ed-59a1-5429-9c88-30a68cc3b4dc" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 =-\\frac{3}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082410} {:answer/id #uuid "0a46d561-b2f3-58d4-94a4-01099db3bb41" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}(x + 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082411} {:answer/id #uuid "1068a7ae-c66e-5103-a69c-cd043a388acd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 =-\\frac{5}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082412} {:answer/id #uuid "383e37b1-7785-5316-993d-06f2ecc4bf50" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y - 1 =-\\frac{5}{2}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082413} {:answer/id #uuid "3a3d43fe-583d-5c3c-b161-e97547fb6627" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y + 1 = \\frac{5}{4}(x - 3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082414}] :answer-field/id #uuid "dce6874e-77a5-5de0-abb5-e4403a900937" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186082409}] :question/id #uuid "c6d49020-9a4a-5e6f-8526-58e27d438cd8" :question/math-academy-id "q-6862" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n$x = t^{2} - 2t, y = t^{5}, - ∞ < t < ∞$.\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t =-1$.\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186256700 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0f2483de-bb9b-57f7-a79f-34885e11205d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186253803} {:answer/id #uuid "c65aceae-e84b-5651-9ab5-ee308d3662ae" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\frac{\\pi }{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255443} {:answer/id #uuid "cb258778-9d8d-556c-8e5b-b5614a8787a6" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\pi )" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255476} {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} {:answer/id #uuid "fb0c7cf4-bb80-57c1-a9c1-3db0dee8005d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{\\pi }{6})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255874}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f09c891f-e284-5b41-96e4-62a818b69c4f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186255773} :answer-field/id #uuid "54d4061c-5bca-5874-a889-0e5b0bcb0553" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186256085}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "405e30d2-1994-59ac-94e7-5e7998f82913" :question/math-academy-id "q-122698" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={e}^{3t}+1,\\,y={e}^{t}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t),\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=0$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=0$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={e}^{3\\cdot 0}+1=1+1=2, \\\\ y & ={e}^{0}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=0\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,0).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{e}^{3t},\\,{y}^{′}(t)={e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)),\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{t}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t))}{3{e}^{3t}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi t)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi t)}{3{e}^{2t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=0,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=0} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)+\\pi \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)}{3{e}^{2\\cdot 0}} \\\\  & =\\frac{0+\\pi }{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,0)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{\\pi }{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(x-2).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-0 & =\\frac{\\pi }{3}(0-2) \\\\ y & =-\\frac{2\\pi }{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,-\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ is the $y$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186275337 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "072d7761-9781-53c7-9bbc-78084d322e9d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{9},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272450} {:answer/id #uuid "3dffbfb9-f270-5fee-bafd-93e2af0b9661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} {:answer/id #uuid "5d3a5947-d436-56d5-880b-0465859f1964" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{7},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273188} {:answer/id #uuid "80184777-1859-51fd-907f-4bb3c159a4d9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{2}{5},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186273486} {:answer/id #uuid "e955007e-17f7-5db4-9fbf-cecc47ec6559" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{4},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274451}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3dffbfb9-f270-5fee-bafd-93e2af0b9661" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186272918} :answer-field/id #uuid "affbb4e4-fcfc-5f64-b6b5-e473fd4f7c9b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186274941}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "51cd744a-a0db-52ff-b937-07f4a84310d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-15564" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{sin}⁡t+\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+\\frac{1}{3},\\,y=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $x$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+\\frac{1}{3}=1+0+\\frac{1}{3}=\\frac{4}{3}, \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=0+1=1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(\\frac{4}{3},1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t(\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡t-\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡t-\\operatorname{cos}⁡t}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=\\frac{\\pi }{2},$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=\\pi /2} & =\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1-0} \\\\  & =1\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\frac{4}{3},1)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $x$-axis when $y=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}0-1 & =1\\cdot (x-\\frac{4}{3}) \\\\ -1 & =x-\\frac{4}{3} \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(\\frac{1}{3},\\,0)$ is the $x$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319229 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f71379ca-dfb4-5d56-adba-c3c3f3d771b7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "x+9" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318382} :answer-field/id #uuid "4287acad-245f-58bd-be4f-6dbce3ede11e" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318564}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9181afbc-9550-51c6-b28c-f82450210472" :question/math-academy-id "q-348109" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{3}-4t,\\,y=2{t}^{2}+1,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=2.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{3}-4(2)=8-8=0 \\\\ y & =2(2)^{2}+1=2(4)+1=9\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(0,9).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}-4,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=4t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{4t}{3{t}^{2}-4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,2} & =\\frac{4(2)}{3(2)^{2}-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{3(4)-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{12-4} \\\\  & =\\frac{8}{8} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(0,9)$ and the slope $m=1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-9 & =1(x-0) \\\\ y-9 & =x \\\\ y & =x+9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=x+9.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186319230 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5a97d047-8132-5364-9050-62b56b8464f9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317873} :answer-field/id #uuid "1bbbd214-a055-53f2-b3b5-67bd9aed6a47" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318484} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5bd25ab4-278a-5d16-bdb6-ac73bab524da" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186317877} :answer-field/id #uuid "ee152229-0df5-5943-83fa-bcee1ecff76b" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186318920}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "9a109daf-d742-5b15-87da-27961e643b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-348115" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+2),\\,y=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t,\\,t\\in (0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=e$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=e$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)=3e \\\\ y & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡e=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(3e,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{t}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{t(\\operatorname{ln}⁡t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=e,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,e} & =\\frac{1}{e(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4e}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(3e,1)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{4e}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1=\\frac{1}{4e}(x-3e).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =\\frac{1}{4e}(0-3e) \\\\ y-1 & =-\\frac{3}{4} \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(0,\\,\\frac{1}{4}).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527941 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4788896b-351b-534b-9161-6b8198197280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527446}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4788896b-351b-534b-9161-6b8198197280" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-x-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527446} :answer-field/id #uuid "30e14e4c-2726-5102-a2db-8f810931d2d9" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527818}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "52b01e00-7882-5db4-abc3-b8cfaaac3472" :question/math-academy-id "q-350328" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{5}-4{t}^{3},\\,y={t}^{2},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nFind the Cartesian equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$\n\nThe Cartesian equation of the tangent line is $y=$\n{{field-1}}." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2({1}^{5})-4({1}^{3})=2-4=-2 \\\\ y & ={1}^{2}=1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(-2,1).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=2t\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{2t}{10{t}^{4}-12{t}^{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,1} & =\\frac{2(1)}{10({1}^{4})-12({1}^{2})} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{10-12} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(-2)} \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the Cartesian equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(-2,1)$ and the slope $m=-1.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-1 & =-1(x-(-2)) \\\\ y-1 & =-(x+2) \\\\ y & =-x-2+1 \\\\ y & =-x-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore the equation of the tangent line  in slope-intercept form is $y=-x-1.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1c9eaf20-c666-5150-bbf7-5cdd25f3858a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527371} {:answer/id #uuid "23a36e5a-2d17-540d-a480-934ae5ff5398" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527387} {:answer/id #uuid "51715b4c-e8ae-5dee-8559-fabf91340f14" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+5=2\\sqrt{3}(x+2)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527462} {:answer/id #uuid "b8e00b9e-7f09-5e90-bc56-bbe95b5482c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527689} {:answer/id #uuid "fdfe79b9-6624-5d5d-a154-82e31819e4aa" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+\\sqrt{2}=\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{4}(x+5)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527798}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b8e00b9e-7f09-5e90-bc56-bbe95b5482c7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-5=6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527689} :answer-field/id #uuid "856fdf82-8f4f-508d-812e-b4bde879e8dc" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5579ca0e-f803-5d9f-87c0-8c8636fd4e81" :question/math-academy-id "q-122584" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=\\sqrt{t+1},\\,y={t}^{3}+4,\\,-1<t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =\\sqrt{1+1}=\\sqrt{2}, \\\\ y & =(1)^{3}+4=5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(\\sqrt{2},5).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}}{(\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{t+1}})} \\\\  & =6{t}^{2}\\sqrt{(t+1)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =6\\cdot {1}^{2}\\sqrt{1+1} \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(\\sqrt{2},5)$ and the slope $m=6\\sqrt{2}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-5 & =6\\sqrt{2}(x-\\sqrt{2}).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9faef563-301f-5002-ae66-16138801d477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\frac{7}{3}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527636} {:answer/id #uuid "c7de3c3f-b559-579e-a3ef-c39be1c56cc0" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-2=7(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527708} {:answer/id #uuid "e48643fb-0c56-523a-9ebc-a1564cea5906" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\frac{14}{3}(x-3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527757} {:answer/id #uuid "f25addc9-667b-53c2-8974-4909d91dac02" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+2=\\frac{7}{6}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527779} {:answer/id #uuid "f4b72991-09c1-5e53-ab67-51fa799187db" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y+4=\\frac{3}{7}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527784}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9faef563-301f-5002-ae66-16138801d477" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "y-4=\\frac{7}{3}(x-10)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527636} :answer-field/id #uuid "84a0580e-1530-5142-8694-ea6e03c23e1e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "9473c03d-7209-5d2d-815a-9982b2c23f32" :question/math-academy-id "q-75267" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx=2{t}^{2}+8t,\\,y=4{t}^{7},\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nCalculate the equation of the tangent to the curve at the point where $t=1.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point by substituting $t=1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =2(1)^{2}+8(1)=2+8=10, \\\\ y & =4(1)^{7}=4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent at the point $(10,4).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=4t+8,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=28{t}^{6}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{28{t}^{6}}{4t+8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=1} & =\\frac{28\\cdot (1)^{6}}{4\\cdot (1)+8} \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,4)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{7}{3}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =\\frac{7}{3}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527989 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "890682ad-7bd6-5e47-a193-c49b79f9309a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527576} {:answer/id #uuid "8aa24a59-28c0-5ddf-af4a-9ea620581550" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{16}{5})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527581} {:answer/id #uuid "8f1ded1a-3543-58c8-aa2a-3a66aeaf334f" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{5}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527589} {:answer/id #uuid "c456bbd6-ff27-5bbd-b987-50a276c4e8b9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{12}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527699} {:answer/id #uuid "e77c3742-a6e4-525c-89a7-67b1faaea9f7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,-\\frac{2}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527763}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "890682ad-7bd6-5e47-a193-c49b79f9309a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527576} :answer-field/id #uuid "45604a60-69c4-5549-aadb-af6a3764d454" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527831}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c3009bb8-76e4-5e7a-be7f-b556ddcfdaef" :question/math-academy-id "q-122700" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+3t,\\,y=1+\\sqrt{2t},\\,t\\in [0,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=2$ on the curve. Determine the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis." :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=2$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(2)^{2}+3(2)=10, \\\\ y & =1+\\sqrt{2(2)}=3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(10,3).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+3,\\,{y}^{′}(t)=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}}}{2t+3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2t}(2t+3)}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=2,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t=2} & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2\\cdot 2}(2\\cdot 2+3)} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{14}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(10,3)$ and the slope $m=\\frac{1}{14}.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(x-10).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-3 & =\\frac{1}{14}(0-10) \\\\ y-3 & =-\\frac{5}{7} \\\\ y & =\\frac{16}{7}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, $(0,\\,\\frac{16}{7})$ is the $y$-intercept."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186527993 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "145eff6a-ad61-5001-ab14-cf77e93a7958" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18c82db0-9172-53d3-850a-85947c46a5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527358}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "18c82db0-9172-53d3-850a-85947c46a5d8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527358} :answer-field/id #uuid "fc7e3261-08d2-5e99-8825-f84a4ffcfbd5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527907} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "e67e0c17-5d27-54f5-a8e6-a2baf1d7260c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527761}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e67e0c17-5d27-54f5-a8e6-a2baf1d7260c" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527761} :answer-field/id #uuid "ff55a139-4c0c-56e8-a3f1-ea5a3e41bb14" :answer-field/key "field-2" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186527909}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "cdc93f1c-a598-5c34-91ec-6e0adc895b1e" :question/math-academy-id "q-350343" :question/problem "A curve is defined by the parametric equations\n\n$$\nx={t}^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}t+\\frac{3}{2},\\,y={t}^{3}+7{t}^{2}+8t,\\,-\\infty <t<\\infty .\n$$\n\nLet $C$ be the point that corresponds to $t=-1$ on the curve. Determine the coordinates of the point at which the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis. Do not round the answers.\n\nThe tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at $(${{field-1}},\n{{field-2}}$).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we find the coordinates of the point $C$ by substituting $t=-1$ into the parametric equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =(-1)^{2}+\\frac{1}{2}(-1)+\\frac{3}{2}=1-\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{3}{2}=2 \\\\ y & =(-1)^{3}+7(-1)^{2}+8(-1)=-1+7-8=-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we're looking to find the equation of the tangent line to the curve at the point $(2,-2).$ \n\nTo find the equation of the tangent line, we need to find $\\frac{dy}{dx}.$ To compute it, we first need to find the derivatives of $x$ and $y,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=2t+\\frac{1}{2},\\,{y}^{′}(t)=3{t}^{2}+14t+8\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dx} & =\\frac{{y}^{′}(t)}{{x}^{′}(t)} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{t}^{2}+14t+8}{2t+\\frac{1}{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAt the point where $t=-1,$ the slope of the tangent line is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m={\\frac{dy}{dx}|}_{t\\,=\\,-1} & =\\frac{3(-1)^{2}+14(-1)+8}{2(-1)+\\frac{1}{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{(-\\frac{3}{2})} \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, to calculate the equation of the tangent line, we use point-slope form with the point $({x}_{1},{y}_{1})=(2,-2)$ and the slope $m=2.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-(-2) & =2(x-2) \\\\ y+2 & =2x-4 \\\\ y & =2x-4-2 \\\\ y & =2x-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe tangent crosses the $y$-axis when $x=0.$ At this point, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =2(0)-6 \\\\ y & =0-6 \\\\ y & =-6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the tangent to the curve at $C$ crosses the $y$-axis at  $(0,\\,-6).$"}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Tangent Line to a Parametric Curve"}]}
